# Star Grammar

Status: stable

This document defines token syntax and the nested block structure of Star source programs. Control-flow behavior and logical branch-target rules are in `spec/control-flow.md`. `spec/source-format.md` defines UTF-8 validation and character handling. `spec/opcodes.toml` is the authority for which instruction opcodes are assigned.

## Lexical conventions

The lexer validates and normalizes the source before applying this grammar:

- In normal mode, ASCII space, horizontal tab, CR, and LF are discarded, including inside data literals.
- In strict mode, those formatting characters are rejected; only `*`, `'`, and `"` are accepted.
- No comment syntax or other source characters are defined.
- Diagnostics retain positions in the original source bytes as specified by `spec/source-format.md`.

The grammar is applied to the resulting stream of `*`, `'`, and `"`. EBNF uses `|` for alternatives, `{ ... }` for zero or more repetitions, and `[ ... ]` for an optional sequence.

## Token grammar

```ebnf
program                  = { statement } ;

statement                = ordinary_instruction
                         | data_literal
                         | structured_block ;

ordinary_instruction     = basic_instruction
                         | ordinary_extended_instruction ;

basic_instruction       = asterisk, { asterisk }, apostrophe ;

extended_instruction    = apostrophe,
                           bit, bit, bit, bit,
                           bit, bit, bit, bit,
                           double_quote ;

ordinary_extended_instruction = extended_instruction ;

data_literal             = double_quote, bit, { bit }, double_quote ;

bit                      = asterisk | apostrophe ;

asterisk                 = "*" ;
apostrophe               = "'" ;
double_quote             = '"' ;
```

`basic_instruction` is valid only when it contains 1 through 12 asterisks before the apostrophe, matching the basic instructions in `spec/opcodes.toml`. An extended instruction contains exactly eight payload bits, most-significant bit first; apostrophe represents 0 and asterisk represents 1. A data literal has a non-empty bit payload with the same bit mapping. There are no escape sequences.

An `ordinary_extended_instruction` is an `extended_instruction` whose decoded opcode is assigned in `spec/opcodes.toml` and is not one of the structural opcodes below. The grammar intentionally does not copy the full opcode catalog. An unassigned or reserved opcode is not a valid instruction and must be rejected during compilation.

## Structural opcode aliases

The names in the grammar below are aliases for extended instructions with these source opcode values. The eight-bit payload is the value from `spec/opcodes.toml`; it is not a bytecode opcode.

| Grammar terminal | Source opcode | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `IF` | `0x30` | Begin conditional |
| `ELSE` | `0x31` | Separate conditional branches |
| `END` | `0x32` | Close an `IF`, `LOOP`, `FOR`, `FUNC`, `ASYNC_FUNC`, or `TRY` block |
| `LOOP` | `0x33` | Begin loop |
| `WHILE` | `0x34` | Separate loop condition from loop body |
| `FOR` | `0x37` | Begin iterator loop |
| `FUNC` | `0x40` | Begin function definition |
| `SCOPE_BEGIN` | `0x55` | Begin lexical scope |
| `SCOPE_END` | `0x56` | Close lexical scope |
| `TRY` | `0x87` | Begin a protected region |
| `CATCH` | `0x88` | Begin a catch-all handler |
| `FINALLY` | `0x89` | Begin a finalizer |
| `ASYNC_FUNC` | `0xA0` | Begin asynchronous function definition |

These aliases are structural terminals and are excluded from `ordinary_extended_instruction`. Other assigned instructions, including `BREAK`, `CONTINUE`, and `RETURN`, remain ordinary instructions in this grammar.

## Nested block grammar

```ebnf
structured_block         = conditional_block
                         | loop_block
                         | for_block
                         | function_block
                         | async_function_block
                         | try_block
                         | scope_block ;

conditional_block        = IF, { statement }, [ ELSE, { statement } ], END ;

loop_block               = LOOP, { statement }, END
                         | LOOP, statement, { statement }, WHILE,
                           { statement }, END ;

for_block                = FOR, { statement }, END ;

function_block           = FUNC, { statement }, END ;

async_function_block     = ASYNC_FUNC, { statement }, END ;

scope_block              = SCOPE_BEGIN, { statement }, SCOPE_END ;

try_block                = TRY, { statement }, CATCH, { statement },
                           [ FINALLY, { statement } ], END
                         | TRY, { statement }, FINALLY, { statement }, END ;
```

When present, the non-empty statement sequence before `WHILE` produces the loop condition, and the optional sequence after it is the body. Omitting `WHILE` denotes the condition-free loop described in the control-flow specification. `FOR` iterator and variable inputs, and function identifiers and parameter metadata, are established by preceding instructions as described in their respective specifications; function parameter IDs, counts, and the rest-parameter code are defined in `spec/function-abi.md`, and are not grammar-level operands.

## Well-formedness rules

- A program may be empty. A data literal may not have an empty payload.
- Every structural block must use its matching closer. `END` cannot close a lexical scope, and `SCOPE_END` cannot close another block.
- An `IF` may contain at most one `ELSE`; `ELSE` is valid only directly inside its matching `IF`.
- `WHILE` is optional and may occur at most once at the direct level of a `LOOP`; it separates the loop condition from the body. It is not a standalone statement.
- `TRY` must contain at least one direct `CATCH` or `FINALLY`; it may contain one of each, with `CATCH` before `FINALLY`. `CATCH` catches any non-fatal runtime error. The catch handler and finalizer use the `TRY` block's surrounding lexical scope.
- `CATCH` and `FINALLY` are valid only directly within their matching `TRY`; they cannot repeat. `FINALLY` is also valid without `CATCH`.
- `END` and `SCOPE_END` are closers, not standalone statements. A block opener without its matching closer is invalid.
- `BREAK` and `CONTINUE` are syntactically ordinary instructions; they are valid only within a loop. Their placement is checked during semantic validation.
- Each `ordinary_extended_instruction` must resolve to an assigned source opcode. Syntax alone does not make reserved or pending opcodes valid.
- The object specification assigns struct, method, and interface opcodes but defines no block delimiters for them. They remain ordinary instruction tokens until their source syntax is specified.
- Historical VM candidates `0x80-0x86` remain unassigned. Source exception aliases use `0x87-0x89` and remain separate from the `.starc` bytecode namespace.

